{%- comment -%}
  Florista buy-box add-on rows — the owner chose "checkbox rows", 2026-08-28.
  Selection made smarter 2026-08-30. See docs/design-log.md.

  Two tappable rows sitting between the delivery note and the add button,
  inside the product form: photo · name · price · checkbox. Ticking a row adds
  that product to the cart as its OWN line item alongside the bouquet
  (florista-atc.js posts an items[] array), and the button's running total
  follows. It is not a line item property — the florist has to pick a real
  bottle off a real shelf.

  WHICH PRODUCTS APPEAR, in priority order:

    1. the three theme-editor pickers, in order, when set — an absolute
       override, nothing below runs;
    2. otherwise the two rows are chosen from three source collections —
       `drinks`, `balloons`, `chocolate` — by the rules below.

  The rules, and why each exists:

    · NEVER THE CATEGORY THE PRODUCT ALREADY IS. A wine page offering another
      wine is noise; a chocolate bar offering a chocolate bar is worse. The
      product's own category drops out of the running, leaving two of three.
    · OCCASION MATCH. When the product reads as a birthday item — it sits in
      `birthday`, or its title/type says so — the balloon row is restricted to
      balloons whose title carries the occasion (יום הולדת / Happy Birthday).
      The balloon titles already name their occasion in plain Hebrew, so this
      needs no tagging pass. If the restriction leaves nothing, it is dropped
      rather than shown empty.
    · PRICE SANITY. An add-on costing more than 40% of the product is a big ask
      standing next to the add button — a 74.99 bottle beside a 159.99 bouquet
      reads as a second purchase, not a finishing touch. Over-cap add-ons are
      dropped, unless dropping them empties the pool, in which case the cap is
      ignored rather than the row lost.
    · ROTATION. What survives is indexed by `product.id | modulo: size`, so
      different products show different add-ons — but the SAME product always
      shows the same pair. Deterministic on purpose: it is cache-safe, it does
      not flicker between visits, and it can be reasoned about when the owner asks
      why a given page shows a given bottle. Liquid has no `rand` anyway.

  Skipped throughout: the product being viewed (never offer itself), anything
  unavailable, and any product with more than one variant — a multi-variant
  add-on would need a choice this row has no room to ask for (the digit
  balloons are the live example: they need an age).

  Renders nothing at all when that leaves no rows. Starts `hidden` and is
  unhidden by florista-atc.js, so a no-JS visitor is never shown a checkbox
  that cannot do anything.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  One `product_list` rather than three `product` settings: a section schema
  accepts the `product` type only ONCE, and three of them make the whole
  section fail to upload ("setting product type can only be inserted once in
  the settings"). Theme Check does not catch it — the theme dev server does,
  by refusing the file. Found 2026-08-28.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign addon_handles = '' -%}
{%- for picked in section.settings.addon_products -%}
  {%- assign addon_handles = addon_handles | append: picked.handle | append: ',' -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- if addon_handles == blank -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The product's own collection handles, comma-fenced on both ends so a
    `contains ',balloons,'` test cannot half-match a longer handle.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign own_cols = ',' -%}
  {%- for c in product.collections -%}
    {%- assign own_cols = own_cols | append: c.handle | append: ',' -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Occasion. Only birthday is detectable today: `birthday` is the one
    occasion collection in the catalog, and `rosh-hashana` holds the seasonal
    chocolate bars — which are add-ons themselves, not products that need one.
    Add a case here when a real occasion collection (love, get-well, sympathy)
    exists to key off.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign balloon_key = '' -%}
  {%- if own_cols contains ',birthday,' -%}
    {%- assign balloon_key = 'יום הולדת' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if product.type contains 'יום הולדת' or product.title contains 'יום הולדת' -%}
    {%- assign balloon_key = 'יום הולדת' -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%} 40% of the product price, in cents, same units as p.price. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign price_cap = product.price | times: 40 | divided_by: 100 -%}

  {%- assign cat_handles = 'drinks,balloons,chocolate' | split: ',' -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    One pass per source collection, building four candidate strings, then
    keeping the best that is not blank: occasion+affordable, then occasion,
    then affordable, then everything. Written with flags and `assign` rather
    than `break`/`continue` inside a `capture` — control-flow keywords are not
    trustworthy there, which is how the balloon row silently vanished on
    2026-08-28.

    The segments are joined with a pipe and the bundle is closed with `END`,
    because Liquid's `split` DROPS a trailing empty segment: without the
    sentinel an empty third collection would shorten the array and slide the
    indexes under `pools[2]`.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign pool_bundle = '' -%}
  {%- for ch in cat_handles -%}
    {%- assign p_all = '' -%}
    {%- assign p_match = '' -%}
    {%- assign p_afford = '' -%}
    {%- assign p_match_afford = '' -%}

    {%- for p in collections[ch].products -%}
      {%- if p.available and p.handle != product.handle and p.variants.size == 1 -%}
        {%- assign p_all = p_all | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}

        {%- assign is_afford = false -%}
        {%- if p.price <= price_cap -%}
          {%- assign is_afford = true -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- assign is_match = false -%}
        {%- if balloon_key != blank and ch == 'balloons' -%}
          {%- if p.title contains balloon_key or p.title contains 'Happy Birthday' -%}
            {%- assign is_match = true -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if is_afford -%}
          {%- assign p_afford = p_afford | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if is_match -%}
          {%- assign p_match = p_match | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if is_match and is_afford -%}
          {%- assign p_match_afford = p_match_afford | append: p.handle | append: ',' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- assign p_best = p_all -%}
    {%- if p_afford != blank -%}
      {%- assign p_best = p_afford -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if p_match != blank -%}
      {%- assign p_best = p_match -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if p_match_afford != blank -%}
      {%- assign p_best = p_match_afford -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- assign pool_bundle = pool_bundle | append: p_best | append: '~' -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- assign pools = pool_bundle | append: 'END' | split: '~' -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Which categories are still in the running: not the one the product itself
    belongs to, and not one whose pool came back empty. Stores the INDEX into
    `pools`, not the handle, so the pool can be read back without a lookup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign cats = '' -%}
  {%- for ch in cat_handles -%}
    {%- assign needle = ',' | append: ch | append: ',' -%}
    {%- assign usable = true -%}
    {%- if own_cols contains needle -%}
      {%- assign usable = false -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if pools[forloop.index0] == blank -%}
      {%- assign usable = false -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if usable -%}
      {%- assign cats = cats | append: forloop.index0 | append: ',' -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- assign cat_idx = cats | split: ',' -%}

  {%- if cat_idx.size > 0 -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Rotate WHICH pair of categories is shown as well as which item inside
      each, so two neighbouring products do not just differ by bottle.
      `plus: 0` forces the split's string back to a number — Liquid will not
      index an array with '0'.

      But an occasion match is the strongest signal there is, so when one fired
      the balloon category is PINNED to the first row instead of being left to
      the rotation. Without the pin a birthday product can rotate onto
      wine+chocolate and the matched balloon never appears — which is the whole
      point of matching. Caught in QA on 2026-08-30: two of the twelve birthday
      products showed no balloon at all. Index 1 is `balloons` in `cat_handles`.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign pinned = false -%}
    {%- if balloon_key != blank and cats contains '1,' -%}
      {%- assign pinned = true -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if pinned -%}
      {%- assign idx_a = 1 -%}
      {%- comment -%} The second row rotates over whatever category is left. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign rest = '' -%}
      {%- for i in cat_idx -%}
        {%- unless i == '1' -%}
          {%- assign rest = rest | append: i | append: ',' -%}
        {%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- assign rest_idx = rest | split: ',' -%}
      {%- assign idx_b = 1 -%}
      {%- if rest_idx.size > 0 -%}
        {%- assign off_b = product.id | modulo: rest_idx.size -%}
        {%- assign idx_b = rest_idx[off_b] | plus: 0 -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- assign off_a = product.id | modulo: cat_idx.size -%}
      {%- assign off_b = off_a | plus: 1 | modulo: cat_idx.size -%}
      {%- assign idx_a = cat_idx[off_a] | plus: 0 -%}
      {%- assign idx_b = cat_idx[off_b] | plus: 0 -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- assign pool_a = pools[idx_a] | split: ',' -%}
    {%- assign pool_b = pools[idx_b] | split: ',' -%}

    {%- assign pick_a = product.id | modulo: pool_a.size -%}
    {%- assign pick_b = product.id | modulo: pool_b.size -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Only one category survived (a balloon page with no wine and no chocolate,
      say) — both rows come from the same pool, so the second row has to step
      one along or the same product renders twice.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if idx_a == idx_b -%}
      {%- assign pick_b = pick_a | plus: 1 | modulo: pool_b.size -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- assign handle_a = pool_a[pick_a] -%}
    {%- assign handle_b = pool_b[pick_b] -%}
    {%- if handle_b == handle_a -%}
      {%- assign handle_b = '' -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- capture addon_handles -%}{{ handle_a }},{{ handle_b }}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign addon_handles = addon_handles | strip -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- assign addon_list = addon_handles | split: ',' -%}

{%- capture addon_rows -%}
  {%- for handle in addon_list -%}
    {%- assign addon = all_products[handle] -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Nested `if` rather than `continue` for the same reason as the `break`
      above — control-flow keywords are not trustworthy inside a capture.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if addon != blank
      and addon.handle != product.handle
      and addon.available
      and addon.variants.size == 1
    -%}
    {%- assign addon_variant = addon.selected_or_first_available_variant -%}
    {%- assign addon_alt = addon.featured_media.alt | default: addon.title -%}
    <li class="fp-addon">
      <label class="fp-addon-row" for="Addon-{{ section.id }}-{{ addon_variant.id }}">
        {%- if addon.featured_media -%}
          {{
            addon.featured_media
            | image_url: width: 168
            | image_tag:
              widths: '56, 112, 168',
              sizes: '56px',
              loading: 'lazy',
              class: 'fp-addon-thumb',
              alt: addon_alt
          }}
        {%- else -%}
          <span class="fp-addon-thumb fp-addon-thumb--empty" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {%- endif -%}

        <span class="fp-addon-text">
          <span class="fp-addon-name">{{ addon.title }}</span>
          {%- if addon.type != blank -%}
            <span class="fp-addon-type">{{ addon.type }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </span>

        <span class="fp-addon-price"><bdi>{{ addon_variant.price | money }}</bdi></span>

        {%- comment -%}
          Deliberately has no `name`: it must never reach Shopify as a form
          field. florista-atc.js reads the data- attributes and builds the
          items[] array itself, so a product with no add-ons ticked posts
          exactly the FormData it posted before this brick existed.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input
          type="checkbox"
          class="fp-addon-check"
          id="Addon-{{ section.id }}-{{ addon_variant.id }}"
          data-addon-id="{{ addon_variant.id }}"
          data-addon-price="{{ addon_variant.price }}"
          data-addon-title="{{ addon.title | escape }}"
        >
      </label>
    </li>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- if addon_rows contains '<li' -%}
  {%- assign addons_heading = section.settings.addons_heading | default: 'להשלמת המתנה' -%}
  <div class="fp-addons" data-addons role="group" aria-labelledby="AddonsHead-{{ section.id }}" hidden>
    <p class="fp-addons-head" id="AddonsHead-{{ section.id }}">{{ addons_heading }}</p>
    <ul class="fp-addons-list" role="list">
      {{ addon_rows }}
    </ul>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
